    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im a pastry chef and im having some issues with my dark chocolate batches. i'm seeing a lot of fat bloom on the surface of the finished bars after they've set. can you explain the science behind why this happens and what i should be looking at in my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom is a common issue in chocolate, where the surface of the chocolate appears to be 'blooming' or 'seeding' due to the formation of chocolate crystal structures that are not in the desired Tempering equilibrium. This occurs when the chocolate's fat molecules are not properly aligned, often caused by incorrect tempering. Look for a lack of medium crystals during tempering, as this can lead to the formation of unwanted crystal structures on the surface. To rectify this, try ensuring your chocolate is heated to the correct temperature (around 105-115°F) and cooled slowly to around 80-82°F before working.",
